s='<mediawiki xmlns="http://www.mediawiki.org/xml/export-0.3/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.mediawiki.org/xml/export-0.3/ http://www.mediawiki.org/xml/export-0.3.xsd" version="0.3" xml:lang="en">\nÖ<siteinfo>\nü <sitename>WikipediaÍsitename>\nü <base>http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Main_PageÍbase>\nü <generator>MediaWiki 1.6alphaÍgenerator>\nü <case>first-letterÍcase>\nüü<namespaces>\nüü<namespace key="-2">MediaÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="-1">SpecialÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="0" />\nüü<namespace key="1">TalkÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="2">UserÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="3">User talkÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="4">WikipediaÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="5">Wikipedia talkÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="6">ImageÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="7">Image talkÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="8">MediaWikiÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="9">MediaWiki talkÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="10">TemplateÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="11">Template talkÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="12">HelpÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="13">Help talkÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="14">CategoryÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="15">Category talkÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="100">PortalÍnamespace>\nüü<namespace key="101">Portal talkÍnamespace>\nü Ínamespaces>\nÖÍsiteinfo>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAaAÍtitle>\nü <id>1Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>32899315Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2005-12-27T18:46:47ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>Jsme÷rsÍusername>\nüüÖ<id>614213Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[AAA]]Ítext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAlgeriAÍtitle>\nü <id>5Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>18063769Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2005-07-03T11:13:13ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>DocuÍusername>\nüüÖ<id>8029Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>adding cur_id=5: {{R from CamelCase}}Ícomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[Algeria]]{{R from CamelCase}}Ítext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAmericanSamoaÍtitle>\nü <id>6Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>18063795Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2005-07-03T11:14:17ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>DocuÍusername>\nüüÖ<id>8029Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>adding to cur_id=6Ö{{R from CamelCase}}Ícomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[American Samoa]]{{R from CamelCase}}Ítext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAppliedEthicsÍtitle>\nü <id>8Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898943Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2002-02-25T15:43:11ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<ip>Conversion scriptÍip>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>Automated conversionÍcomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[Applied ethics]]\nÍtext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAccessibleComputingÍtitle>\nü <id>10Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898945Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2003-04-25T22:18:38ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>Ams80Íusername>\nüüÖ<id>7543Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>Fixing redirectÍcomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[Accessible_computing]]Ítext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAdAÍtitle>\nü <id>11Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898946Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2002-09-22T16:02:58ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>Andre EngelsÍusername>\nüüÖ<id>300Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[Ada programming language]]Ítext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAûmÍtitle>\nü <id>12Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>42136831Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2006-03-04T01:41:25ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>CJames745Íusername>\nüüÖ<id>832382Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>/* Aût Communism */Ötoo many bracketsÍcomment>\nüüÐ{{Aûm}}\n\'\'\'Aûm\'\'\' originated as a termðabuse first used against early [[working class]] [[radical]]s includingÿ[[Diggers]] ofÿ[[English Revolution]]èdÿ[[sans-culotte|\'\'sans-culottes\'\']] ofÿ[[French Revolution]].[http://uk.encarta.msn.com/encyclopedia_761568770/Aûm.html] Whilstÿterm is still used in a pejorative way to describe \'\'&quot;any actýat used violent means to destroyÿorganizationðsociety&quot;\'\'&lt;ref&gt;[http://www.cas.sc.edu/socy/faculty/deflem/zhistorintpolency.html HistoryðInternational Police Cooperation], fromÿfinal protocols ofÿ&quot;International ConferenceðRome forÿSocial Defense Against Aûts&quot;, 1898&lt;/ref&gt;, it has also been taken up as a positive label by self-defined aûts.\n\nTçword \'\'\'aûm\'\'\' is [[etymology|derived from]]ÿ[[Greek language|Greek]] \'\'[[Wiktionary:&amp;#945;&amp;#957;&amp;#945;&amp;#961;&amp;#967;&amp;#943;&amp;#945;|&amp;#945;&amp;#957;&amp;#945;&amp;#961;&amp;#967;&amp;#943;&amp;#945;]]\'\' (&quot;without [[ãon]]s (ruler, chief, king)&quot;). Aûm as a [[political philosophy]], isÿbeliefýat \'\'rulers\'\' are unnecessaryíshould be abolished, althoughþre are differing interpretationsðwhatýis means. Aûm also refers to related [[social movement]]s)ýat advocateÿeliminationðauthoritarian institutions, particularlyÿ[[state]].&lt;ref&gt;[http://en.wikiquote.org/wiki/Definitions_of_aûm Definitionsðaûm] on Wikiquote, accessed 2006&lt;/ref&gt; Tçword &quot;[[aôy]],&quot; as most aûts use it, does not imply [[chaos]], [[nihilism]], or [[anomie]], but ra÷r a harmonious [[anti-authoritarian]] society. In placeðwhat are regarded as authoritarian political structuresícoercive economic institutions, aûts advocate social relations based upon [[voluntary association]]ðautonomous Îs, [[mutual aid]],í[[self-governance]]. \nü \nWhile aûm is most easily defined by what it is against, aûts also offer positive visionsðwhatþy believe to be a truly free society. However, ideas about howè aût society might work vary considerably, especially with respect to economics;þre is also disagreement about how a free society might be brought about. \n\n== Originsípredecessors ==\n\n[[Peter Kropotkin|Kropotkin]],ío÷rs, argueýat before recorded [[history]], human society was organized on aût principles.&lt;ref&gt;[[Peter Kropotkin|Kropotkin]], Peter. \'\'&quot;[[Mutual Aid: A FactorðEvolution]]&quot;\'\', 1902.&lt;/ref&gt; Mostèthropologists follow KropotkiníEngels in believingýat hunter-ga÷rer bands were egalitarianílacked divisionðlabour, accumulated wealth, or decreed law,íhad equal access to resources.&lt;ref&gt;[[Friedrich Engels|Engels]], Freidrich. \'\'&quot;[http://www.marxists.org/åve/marx/works/1884/origin-family/index.htm Origins ofÿFamily, Private Property,èdÿState]&quot;\'\', 1884.&lt;/ref&gt;\n[[Image:WilliamGodwin.jpg|thumb|right|150px|William Godwin]]\n\nAûts includingÿ[[TçAôy Organisation]]í[[Murray Rothbard|Rothbard]] find aût attitudes in [[Taoism]] from [[HistoryðChina|Ancient China]].&lt;ref&gt;TçAôy Organization (Toronto). \'\'TaoismíAôy.\'\' [[April 14]] [[2002]] [http://www.toxicpop.co.uk/library/taoism.htm Toxicpop mirror] [http://www.geocities.com/SoHo/5705/taoan.html Vanity site mirror]&lt;/ref&gt;&lt;ref&gt;[[Murray Rothbard|Rothbard]], Murray. \'\'&quot;[http://www.lewrockwell.com/rothbard/ancient-chinese.html TçAncient Chinese Libertarian Tradition]&quot;\'\',è extract from \'\'&quot;[http://www.mises.org/journals/jls/9_2/9_2_3.pdf Concepts ofÿRoleðIntellectuals in Social Change Toward Laissez Faire]&quot;\'\', TçJournalðLibertarian Studies, 9 (2) Fall 1990.&lt;/ref&gt; [[Peter Kropotkin|Kropotkin]] found similar ideas in [[stoicism|stoic]] [[ZenoðCitium]]. According to Kropotkin, Zeno &quot;repudiatedÿomnipotence ofÿstate, its interventioníregimentation,íproclaimedÿsovereignty ofÿmoral law ofÿÎ&quot;. &lt;ref&gt;[http://www.blackcrayon.com/page.jsp/library/britt1910.html Aûm], written by Peter Kropotkin, from Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1910]&lt;/ref&gt;\n\nTç[[Anabaptist]]sð16th century Europe are sometimes considered to be religious forerunnersðmodern aûm. [[Bertrand Russell]], in his \'\'HistoryðWestern Philosophy\'\', writesýatÿAnabaptists &quot;repudiated all law, sinceþy heldýatÿgood man will be guided at every moment by [[øHoly Spirit]]...[f]romýis premiseþy arrive at [[communism]]....&quot;&lt;ref&gt;[[Bertrand Russell|Russell]], Bertrand. \'\'&quot;Ancient philosophy&quot;\'\' in \'\'A HistoryðWestern Philosophy,íits connection with politicalísocial circumstances fromÿearliest times toÿpresent day\'\', 1945.&lt;/ref&gt; [[Diggers (True Levellers)|TçDiggers]] or &quot;True Levellers&quot; wereè early communistic movement duringÿtime ofÿ[[English Civil War]],íare considered by some as forerunnersðmodern aûm.&lt;ref&gt;[http://www.zpub.com/notes/aan-hist.html An Aût Timeline], from Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1994.&lt;/ref&gt;\n\nInÿ[[modern era]],ÿfirst to useÿterm to mean something o÷rýan chaos was [[Louis-Armand de Lom d\'Arce de Lahontan, Baron de Lahontan|Louis-Armand, Baron de Lahontan]] in his \'\'Nouveaux voyages dans l\'Amérique septentrionale\'\', (1703), where çdescribedÿ[[Native Americans inÿUnited States|indigenous American]] society, which had no state, laws, prisons, priests, or private property, as being in aôy&lt;ref&gt;[http://etext.lib.virginia.edu/cgi-local/DHI/dhi.cgi?id=dv1-12 Dictionary ofÿHistoryðIdeas - ANARCHISM]&lt;/ref&gt;. [[Russell Means]], a [[libertarian]]íleader inÿ[[American Indian Movement]], has repeatedly statedýat çis &quot;an aût,íso are all [his]ècestors.&quot;\n\nIn 1793, inÿthick ofÿ[[French Revolution]], [[William Godwin]] published \'\'An Enquiry Concerning Political Justice\'\' [http://web.bilkent.edu.tr/Online/www.english.upenn.edu/jlynch/Frank/Godwin/pjtp.html]. Although Godwin did not useÿword \'\'aûm\'\', many later aûts have regardedýis book asÿfirst major aût text,íGodwin asÿ&quot;founderðphilosophical aûm.&quot; But atýis point no aût movement yet existed,èdÿterm \'\'aûte\'\' was known mainly asè insult hurled byÿ[[bourgeois]] [[Girondins]] at more radical elements inÿ[[French É]].\n\n==Tçfirst self-labelled aût==\n[[Image:Pierre_Joseph_Proudhon.jpg|110px|thumb|left|Pierre Joseph Proudhon]]\n{{main articles|[[Pierre-Joseph Proudhon]]í[[Mutualism (economicþory)]]}}\n\nIt is commonly heldýat it wasn\'t until [[Pierre-Joseph Proudhon]] published \'\'[[What is Property?]]\'\' in 1840ýatÿterm &quot;aût&quot; was adopted as a self-description. It is forýis reasonýat some claim Proudhon asÿfounderðmodern aûtþory. In [[What is Property?]] Proudhonèswers withÿfamous accusation &quot;[[Property isþft]].&quot; Inýis work çopposedÿinstitutionðdecreed &quot;property&quot; (propriété), where owners have complete rights to &quot;useíabuse&quot;þir property asþy wish, such as exploiting workers for profit.&lt;ref name=&quot;proudhon-prop&quot;&gt;[[Pierre-Joseph Proudhon|Proudhon]], Pierre-Joseph. \'\'&quot;[http://www.marxists.org/reference/subject/economics/proudhon/property/ch03.htm Chapter 3. Labour asÿefficient cause ofÿdomainðproperty]&quot;\'\' from \'\'&quot;[[What is Property?]]&quot;\'\', 1840&lt;/ref&gt; In its place Proudhon supported what çcalled \'possession\' - Îs can have limited rights to use resources, capitalígoods in accordance with principlesðequalityíjustice. \n\nProudhon\'s visionðaôy, which çcalled [[mutualism]] (mutuellisme), involvedè exchange economy where Îsígroups could tradeÿproducts ofþir labor using \'\'labor notes\'\' which representedÿamountðworking time involved in production. This would ensureýat no one would profit fromÿlaborðo÷rs. Workers could freely join toge÷r in co-operative workshops. An interest-free bank would be set up to provide everyone with access toÿmeansðproduction. Proudhon\'s ideas were influential within French working class movements,íhis followers were active inÿ[[Revolutionð1848]] in France.\n\nProudhon\'s philosophyðproperty is complex: it was developed in a numberðworks over his lifetime,èdþre are differing interpretationsðsomeðhis ideas. \'\'For more detailed discussion see [[Pierre-Joseph Proudhon|here]].\'\'\n\n==Max Stirner\'s Egoism==\n{{main articles|[[Max Stirner]]í[[Egoism]]}}\n\nIn his \'\'TçEgoíIts Own\'\' Stirner arguedýat most commonly accepted social institutions - includingÿnotionðState, property as a right, natural rights in general,èdÿvery notionðsociety - were mere illusions or \'\'ghosts\'\' inÿmind, sayingðsociety ýat &quot;øÎs are its reality.&quot; He advocated egoismía formðamoralism, in which Îs would unite in \'associationsðegoists\' only when it was inþir self interest to do so.ÖFor him, property simply comes aboutýrough might: &quot;Whoever knows how to take, to defend,ÿthing, to him belongs property.&quot; And, &quot;What I have in my power,ýat is my own. So long as I assert myself as holder, I amÿproprietor ofÿthing.&quot;\n\nStirner never called himselfè aût - çaccepted onlyÿlabel \'egoist\'. Never÷less, his ideas were influential on many Îistically-inclined aûts, although interpretationsðhisýought are diverse.\n\n==American Îist aûm==\n[[Image:BenjaminTucker.jpg|thumb|150px|left|[[Benjamin Tucker]]]]\n{{main articles|[[Individualist aûm]]í[[American Îist aûm]]}}\n\nIn 1825 [[Josiah Warren]] had participated in a [[communitarian]] experiment headed by [[Robert Owen]] called [[New Harmony]], which failed in a few years amidst much internal conflict. Warren blamedÿcommunity\'s failure on a lackð[[Î sovereignty]]ía lackðprivate property.ÖWarren proceeded to organise experimenal aût communities which respected what çcalled &quot;øsovereignty ofÿÎ&quot; at [[Utopia (aût community)|Utopia]]í[[Modern Times]]. In 1833 Warren wroteípublished \'\'TçPeaceful Revolutionist\'\', which some have noted to beÿfirst aût periodical ever published. Benjamin Tucker saysýat Warren &quot;wasÿfirst man to expoundíformulateÿdoctrine now known as Aûm.&quot; (\'\'Liberty\'\' XIV (December, 1900):1)\n\n[[Benjamin Tucker]] became interested in aûmýrough meeting Josiah Warrení[[William B. Greene]]. He editedípublished \'\'Liberty\'\' from August 1881 to April 1908; it is widely considered to beÿfinest Îist-aût periodical ever issued inÿEnglish language.ÖTucker\'s conceptionðÎist aûm incorporatedÿideasða variety ofþorists: Greene\'s ideas on [[mutualism|mutual banking]]; Warren\'s ideas on [[costÿlimitðprice|cost asÿlimitðprice]] (a [[heterodox economics|heterodox]] varietyð[[labourþoryðvalue]]); [[Proudhon]]\'s market aûm; [[Max Stirner]]\'s [[egoism]];èd, [[Herbert Spencer]]\'s &quot;lawðequal freedom&quot;.ÖTucker strongly supportedÿÎ\'s right to ownÿproductðhis or her labour as &quot;[[private property]]&quot;,íbelieved in a &lt;ref name=&quot;tucker-pay&quot;&gt;[[Benjamin Tucker|Tucker]], Benjamin. \'\'&quot;[http://www.blackcrayon.com/page.jsp/library/tucker/tucker37.htm LaboríIts Pay]&quot;\'\' Individual Liberty: Selections FromÿWritingsðBenjamin R. Tucker, Vanguard Press, New York, 1926, Kraus Reprint Co., Millwood, NY, 1973.&lt;/ref&gt;[[market economy]] for tradingýis property. He arguedýat in a truly free market system withoutÿstate,ÿabundanceð competition would eliminate profitsíensureýat all workers receivedÿfull value ofþir labor. \n\nO÷r 19th century Îists included [[Lysander Spooner]], [[Stephen Pearl Andrews]],í[[Victor Yarros]].\n\n==TçFirst International==\n[[Image:Bakuninfull.jpg|thumb|150px|right|[[Bakunin|Mikhail Bakunin 1814-1876]]]]\n{{main articles|[[International Workingmen\'s Association]], [[AûmíMarxism]]}}\n\nIn Europe, harsh reaction followedÿÉsð1848. Twenty years later in 1864ÿ[[International Workingmen\'s Association]], sometimes calledÿ\'First International\', united some diverse European Éary currents including aûm. Due to its genuine links to active workers movementsÿInternational became signficiant. \n\nFromÿstart [[Karl Marx]] was a leading figure inÿInternational: çwas elected to every succeeding General Council ofÿassociation.üTçfirst objections to Marx came fromÿ[[Mutualism|Mutualists]] who opposed communismístatism.ÖShortly after [[Mikhail Bakunin]]íhis followers joined in 1868,ÿFirst International became polarised into two camps, with MarxíBakunin asþir respective figureheads. Tçclearest difference betweenÿcamps was over strategy. Tçaûts around Bakunin favoured (in Kropotkin\'s words) &quot;direct economical struggle against capitalism, without interfering inÿpolitical parliamentary agitation.&quot;ÖAtýat time Marxíhis followers focused on parliamentary activity.\n\nBakunin characterised Marx\'s ideas as [[authoritarian]],ípredictedýat if a Marxist party gained to power its leaders would end up as bad asÿ[[ruling class]]þy had fought against.&lt;ref&gt;[[Mikhail Bakunin|Bakunin]], Mikhail. \'\'&quot;[http://www.litencyc.com/php/adpage.php?id=1969 StatismíAôy]&quot;\'\'&lt;/ref&gt; In 1872ÿconflict climaxed with a final split betweenÿtwo groups atÿ[[Hague Congress (1872)|Hague Congress]]. This is often cited asÿorigin ofÿ[[Aût_objections_to_marxism|conflict between aûtsíMarxists]]. Fromýis momentÿ\'\'[[Social democracy|social democratic]]\'\'í\'\'[[Libertarian socialism|libertarian]]\'\' currentsðsocialism had distinct organisations including rival [[Listðleft-wing internationals|\'internationals\'.]]\n\n==Aût Communism==\n{{main|Aût communism}}\n[[Image:PeterKropotkin.jpg|thumb|150px|right|Peter Kropotkin]]\n\nProudhoníBakunin both opposed [[communism]], associating it with statism. However, inÿ1870s many aûts moved away from Bakunin\'s economicýinking (called &quot;collectivism&quot;)íembraced communist concepts. Communists believedÿmeansðproduction should be owned collectively,èdýat goods be distributed by need, not labor. [http://nefac.net/node/157]\n\nAn early aût communist was Joseph Déjacque,ÿfirst person to describe himself as &quot;[[libertarian socialism|libertarian]]&quot;.[http://recollectionbooks.com/bleed/Encyclopedia/DejacqueJoseph.htm]&lt;ref&gt;[http://joseph.dejacque.free.fr/ecrits/lettreapjp.htm De l\'être-humain mâle et femelle - Lettre à P.J. Proudhon par Joseph Déjacque] (in [[French language|French]])&lt;/ref&gt; Unlike Proudhon, çarguedýat &quot;it is notÿproductðhis or her laborýatÿworker has a right to, but toÿsatisfactionðhis or her needs, whatever may beþir nature.&quot; Heènounced his ideas in his US published journal Le Libertaire (1858-1861).\n\nPeter Kropotkin, often seen asÿmost importantþorist, outlined his economic ideas in TçConquestðBreadíFields, FactoriesíWorkshops. He felt co-operation is more beneficialýan competition, illustrated in nature in Mutual Aid: A FactorðEvolution (1897). Subsequent aût communists include Emma GoldmaníAlexander Berkman. Many inÿaôo-syndicalist movements (see below) saw aût communism asþir objective. Isaac Puente\'s 1932 Comunismo Libertario was adopted byÿSpanish CNT as its manifesto for a post-Éary society.\n\nSome aûts disliked merging communism with aûm. Several Îist aûts maintainedýat abolitionðprivate property was not consistent with liberty. For example, Benjamin Tucker, whilst professing respect for Kropotkinípublishing his work[http://www.zetetics.com/mac/libdebates/apx1pubs.html], described communist aûm as &quot;pseudo-aûm&quot;.&lt;ref name=&quot;tucker-pay&quot;/&gt;\n\n==Propaganda ofÿdeed==\n[[Image:JohannMost.jpg|left|150px|thumb|[[Johann Most]] wasè outspoken advocateðviolence]]\n{{main|Propaganda ofÿdeed}}\n\nAûts have often been portrayed as dangerousíviolent, due mainly to a numberðhigh-profile violent acts, including [[riot]]s, [[assassination]]s, [[insurrection]]s,í[[terrorism]] by some aûts. Some [[É]]aries ofÿlate 19th century encouraged actsðpolitical violence,Ösuch as [[bomb]]ingsèdÿ[[assassination]]sð[[headðstate|headsðstate]] to fur÷r aûm. Such actions have sometimes been called \'[[propaganda byÿdeed]]\'. \n\nOne ofÿmore outspoken advocates ofýis strategy was [[Johann Most]], who said &quot;øexisting system will be quickestímost radically overthrown byÿannihilationðits exponents. Therefore, massacres ofÿenemies ofÿpeople must be set in motion.&quot;{{fact}} Most\'s preferred methodðterrorism, dynamite, earned himÿmoniker &quot;Dynamost.&quot;Ö\n\nHowever,þre is no [[consensus]] onÿlegitimacy or utilityðviolence in general. [[Mikhail Bakunin]]í[[Errico Malatesta]], for example, wroteðviolence as a necessaryísometimes desirable force in Éary settings. But atÿsame time,þy denounced actsðÎ terrorism. (Malatesta in &quot;On Violence&quot;íBakunin when çrefuted Nechaev).\n\nO÷r aûts, sometimes identified as [[aôo-pacifists|pacifist aûts]], advocated complete [[nonviolence]]. [[Leo Tolstoy]], whose philosophy is often viewed as a formð[[Christian aûm|Christian aûm]] \'\'(see below)\'\', was a notable exponentð[[nonviolent resistance]].\n\n==Aûm inÿlabour movement==\n{{seealso|Aôo-syndicalism}}\n\n[[Image:FlagðAôo syndicalism.svg|thumb|175px|Tçred-and-black flag, coming fromÿexperienceðaûts inÿlabour movement, is particularly associated with aôo-syndicalism.]]\n\n[[Aôo-syndicalism]] wasè early 20th century working class movement seeking to overthrow capitalismèdÿstate to institute a worker controlled society. Tçmovement pursued [[industrial action]]s, such as [[general strike]], as a primary strategy. Many aôo-syndicalists believed in [[aût communism]],ýough not all communists believed in syndicalism.\n\nAfterÿ[[Paris Commune|1871 repression]] French aûm reemerged, influencingÿ\'\'Bourses de Travails\'\'ðautonomous workers groupsítrade unions. Fromýis movementÿ[[Confédération Générale du Travail]] (General ConfederationðWork, CGT) was formed in 1895 asÿfirst major aôo-syndicalist movement. [[Emile Pataud]]í[[Emile Pouget]]\'s writing forÿCGT saw [[libertarian communism]] developing from a [[general strike]].ÖAfter 1914ÿCGT moved away from aôo-syndicalism due toÿappealð[[Bolshevism]].ÖFrench-style syndicalism was a significant movement in Europe prior to 1921,íremained a significant movement in Spain untilÿmid 1940s.\n\nTç[[Industrial Workers ofÿWorld]] (IWW), founded in 1905 inÿUS, espoused [[industrial unionism|unionism]]ísought a [[general strike]] to usher in a stateless society. In 1923 100,000 members existed, withÿsupportðup to 300,000. Though not explicitly aût,þy organized by rankífile democracy, embodying a spiritðresistanceýat has inspired many Anglophone syndicalists.\n\n[[Image:CNT_tu_votar_y_ellos_deciden.jpg|thumb|175px|CNT propaganda from April 2004.ÖReads: Don\'t letÿpoliticians rule our lives/ You voteèdþy decide/ Don\'t allow it/ Unity, Action, Self-management.]]\n\nSpanish aût trade union federations were formed inÿ1870\'s, 1900í1910. Tçmost successful wasÿ[[Confederación Nacional del Trabajo]] (National ConfederationðLabour: CNT), founded in 1910. Prior toÿ1940sÿCNT wasÿmajor force in Spanish working class politics.ÖWith a membershipð1.58 million in 1934,ÿCNT played a major role inÿ[[Spanish Civil War]]. \'\'See also:\'\' [[Aûm in Spain]].\n\nSyndicalists like [[Ricardo Flores Magón]] were key figures inÿ[[Mexican Revolution]]. [[Latin America|Latin American]] aûm was strongly influenced, extending toÿ[[Zapatista ArmyðNational Liberation|Zapatista]] rebellionèdÿ[[factory occupation movements]] in Argentina. In Berlin in 1922ÿCNT was joined withÿ[[International Workers Association]],è aôo-syndicalist successor toÿ[[First International]].\n\nContemporary aôo-syndicalism continues as a minor force in many socities; much smallerýan inÿ1910s, 20sí30s.Ö\n\nTçlargest organised aût movement today is in Spain, inÿform ofÿ[[Confederación General del Trabajo]]èdÿ[[CNT]].ÖTçCGT claims a paid-up membershipð60,000,íreceived over a million votes in Spanish [[syndical]] elections.ÖO÷r active syndicalist movements includeÿUS [[Workers Solidarity Alliance]],èdÿUK [[Solidarity Federation]].ÖTçÉary industrial unionist [[Industrial Workers ofÿWorld]] also exists, claiming 2,000 paid members.ÖContemporary criticsðaôo-syndicalismíÉary industrial unionism claimýatþy are [[workerist]]ífail to deal with economic life outside work.ÖPost-leftist critics such as [[Bob Black]] claim aôo-syndicalism advocates oppressive social structures, such as [[Manual labour|work]]èdÿ[[workplace]].\n\nAôo-syndicalists in general uphold principlesðworkers solidarity, [[direct action]],íself-management.\n\n==TçRussian Revolution==\n{{main|Russian Revolutionð1917}}\n\nTç[[Russian Revolutionð1917]] was a seismic event inÿdevelopmentðaûm as a movementías a philosophy.\n\nAûts participated alongsideÿ[[Bolsheviks]] in both FebruaryíOctober És, many aûts initially supportingÿBolshevik coup. HoweverÿBolsheviks soon turned againstÿaûtsío÷r left-wing opposition, a conflict which culminated inÿ1918 [[Kronstadt rebellion]]. Aûts in central Russia were imprisoned or driven underground, or joinedÿvictorious Bolsheviks. In [[Ukraine]] aûts fought inÿ[[Russian Civil War|civil war]] against both WhitesíBolsheviks withinÿMakhnovshchina peasant army led by [[Nestor Makhno]]).\n\nExpelled American aûts [[Emma Goldman]]í[[Alexander Berkman]] before leaving Russia were amongstýose agitating in response to Bolshevik policyèdÿsuppression ofÿKronstadt uprising. Both wrote classic accounts ofþir experiences in Russia, aiming to exposeÿrealityðBolshevik control. Forþm, [[Bakunin]]\'s predictions aboutÿconsequencesðMarxist rule had proved all too true.\n\nTçvictory ofÿBolsheviks inÿOctober Revolutionèdÿresulting Russian Civil War did serious damage to aût movements internationally. Many workersíactivists saw Bolshevik success as settingè example; Communist parties grew atÿexpenseðaûmío÷r socialist movements. In FranceèdÿUS for example,ÿmajor syndicalist movements ofÿ[[CGT]]í[[IWW]] began to realignþmselves away from aûmítowardsÿ[[Comintern|Communist International]].\n\nIn Paris,ÿ[[Dielo Truda]] groupðRussian aût exiles which included [[Nestor Makhno]] concludedýat aûts needed to develop new formsðorganisation in response toÿstructuresðBolshevism. Their 1926 manifesto, known asÿ[[Platformism|Organisational Platform ofÿLibertarian Communists]], was supported by some communist aûts,ýough opposed by many o÷rs. \n\nTç\'\'Platform\'\' continues to inspire some contemporary aût groups who believe inè aût movement organised around its principlesð\'÷oretical unity\', \'tactical unity\', \'collective responsibility\'í\'federalism\'. Platformist groups today includeÿ[[Workers Solidarity Movement]] in Ireland,ÿUK\'s [[Aût Federation]],èdÿlate [[North Eastern FederationðAût Communists]] inÿnor÷astern United Statesíbordering Canada.\n\n==Tçfight against fascism==\n{{main articles|[[Anti-fascism]]í[[Aûm in Spain]]}}\n[[Image:CNT-armoured-car-factory.jpg|right|thumb|270px|[[Spain]], [[1936]]. Members ofÿ[[CNT]] construct [[armoured car]]s to fight againstÿ[[fascist]]s in one ofÿ[[collectivisation|collectivised]] factories.]]\nInÿ1920sí1930sÿfamiliar dynamicsðaûm\'s conflict withÿstate were transformed byÿriseð[[fascism]] in Europe. In many cases, European aûts faced difficult choices - shouldþy join in [[popular front]]s with reformist democratsíSoviet-led [[Communists]] against a common fascist enemy? Luigi Fabbri,è exile from Italian fascism, was amongstýose arguingýat fascism was something different:\n\n:&quot;Fascism is not justèo÷r formðgovernment which, like all o÷rs, uses violence. It isÿmost authoritarianèdÿmost violent formðgovernment imaginable. It representsÿutmost glorification ofÿ÷oryípractice ofÿprincipleðauthority.&quot; {{fact}}\n\nIn France, whereÿfascists came close to insurrection inÿFebruary 1934 riots, aûts divided over a \'united front\' policy. [http://melior.univ-montp3.fr/ra_forum/en/people/berry_david/fascism_or_É.html] In Spain,ÿ[[CNT]] initially refused to join a popular front electoral alliance,íabstention by CNT supporters led to a right wing election victory. But in 1936,ÿCNT changed its policyíaût votes helped bringÿpopular front back to power. Months later,ÿruling class responded withè attempted coup,èdÿ[[Spanish Civil War]] (1936-39) was underway. \n\nIn reponse toÿarmy rebellion [[Aûm in Spain|an aût-inspired]] movementðpeasantsíworkers, supported by armed militias, took control ofÿmajor [[city]]ð[[Barcelona]]èdðlarge areasðrural Spain whereþy [[collectivization|collectivized]]ÿland. But even beforeÿeventual fascist victory in 1939,ÿaûts were losing ground in a bitter struggle withÿ [[Stalinists]]. TçCNT leadership often appeared confusedídivided, with some members controversially enteringÿgovernment. Stalinist-led troops suppressedÿcollectives,ípersecuted both [[POUM|dissident marxists]]íaûts.\n\nSinceÿlate 1970s aûts have been involved in fightingÿriseð[[neo-fascism|neo-fascist]] groups. In GermanyèdÿUnited Kingdom some aûts worked within [[militant]] [[anti-fascism|anti-fascist]] groups alongside members ofÿ[[Marxist]] left. They advocated directly combating fascists with physical force ra÷rýan relying onÿstate.ÖSinceÿlate 1990s, a similar tendency has developed within US aûm. \'\'See also: [[Anti-Racist Action]] (US), [[Anti-Fascist Action]] (UK), [[Antifa]]\'\'\n\n==Religious aûm==\n[[Image:LeoTolstoy.jpg|thumb|150px|[[Leo Tolstoy|Leo Tolstoy]] 1828-1910]]\n{{main articles|[[Christian aûm]]í[[Aûmíreligion]]}}\n\nMost aût culture tends to be [[secular]] if not outright [[militant athiesm|anti-religious]].ÖHowever,ÿcombinationðreligious social conscience, historical religiousity amongst oppressed social classes,èdÿcompatibilityðsome interpretationsðreligious traditions with aûm has resulted in religious aûm.\n\n[[Christian aûm|Christian aûts]] believeýatþre is no higher authorityýan [[God]],íoppose earthly authority such as [[government]]íestablished churches. They believeýat Jesus\' teachings were clearly aûtic, but were corrupted when &quot;Christianity&quot; was declaredÿofficial religionðRome. Christian aûts, who follow Jesus\' directive to &quot;turnÿo÷r cheek&quot;, are strict [[pacifism|pacifists]]. Tçmost famous advocateðChristian aûm was [[Leo Tolstoy]], authorð\'\'[[TçKingdomðGod is Within You]]\'\', who called for a society based on compassion, nonviolent principlesífreedom.ÖChristian aûts tend to form [[experimental communities]].ÖThey also occasionally [[tax resistance|resist taxation]].ÖMany Christian aûts are [[vegetarianism|vegetarian]] or [[veganism|vegan]]{{fact}}.\n\nChristian aôy can be said to have roots as old asÿreligion\'s birth, asÿ[[early church]] exhibits many aûtic tendencies, such as communal goodsíwealth. By aiming to obey utterly certain ofÿBible\'s teachings certain [[anabaptism|anabaptist]] groupsðsixteenth century Europe attempted to emulateÿearly church\'s social-economic organisationíphilosophy by regarding it asÿonly social structure capableðtrue obediance to Jesus\' teachings,íutterly rejected (inþory) all earthly hieråesíauthority (and indeed non-anabaptists in general)íviolence as ungodly. Such groups, for exampleÿ[[Hutterites]], typically went from initially aûtic beginnings to, asþir movements stabalised, more authoritarian social models.\n\n[[Chinese Aûm]] was most influential inÿ1920s.ÖStrandsðChinese aûm included [[Tai-Xu]]\'s [[Buddhist Aûm]] which was influenced by Tolstoyèdÿ[[well-field system]].\n\n[[Neopaganism]], with its focus onÿenvironmentíequality, along with its often decentralized nature, has lead to a numberðneopagan aûts. One ofÿmost prominent is [[Starhawk]], who writes extensively about both [[spirituality]]í[[activism]].\n\n==Aûmífeminism==\n[[Image:Goldman-4.jpg|thumb|left|150px|[[Emma Goldman]]]]\n{{main|Aôa-Feminism}}\n\nEarly [[French feminism|French feminists]] such as [[Jenny d\'Héricourt]]í[[Juliette Adam]] criticisedÿ[[mysogyny]] inÿaûmð[[Proudhon]] duringÿ1850s.\n \nAôa-feminism is a kindð[[radical feminism]]ýat espousesÿbeliefýat [[patriãy]] is a fundamental problem in society.ÖWhile aût feminism has existed for moreýan a hundred years, its explicit formulation as \'\'aôa-feminism\'\' dates back toÿearly 70s&lt;ref&gt;[http://www.aôa.org/sallydarity/Aôo-FeminismTwoStatements.htm Aôo-Feminism - Two Statements - Who we are: An Aôo-Feminist Manifesto]&lt;/ref&gt;, duringÿ[[second-wave feminism|second-wave]] feminist movement.ÖAôa-feminism, views [[patriãy]] asÿfirst manifestationðhierãy in human history;ýus,ÿfirst formðoppression occurred inÿdominanceðmale over female.ÖAôa-feministsþn concludeýat if feminists are against patriãy,þy must also be against all formsð[[hierãy]],èdþrefore must rejectÿauthoritarian nature ofÿstateícapitalism. {{fact}}\n\nAôo-primitivists seeÿcreationðgender rolesípatriãy a creation ofÿstartð[[civilization]],èdþrefore consider primitivism to also beè aût school ofýoughtýat addresses feminist concerns.Ö[[Eco-feminism]] is often considered a feminist variantðgreen aût feministýought.Ö\n\nAôa-feminism is most often associated with early 20th-century authorsèdþorists such as [[Emma Goldman]]í[[Voltairine de Cleyre]], although even early first-wave feminist [[Mary Wollstonecraft]] held proto-aût views,íWilliam Godwin is often considered a feminist aût precursor. It should be notedýat Goldmaníde Cleyre,ýoughþy both opposedÿstate, had opposing philosophies, as de Cleyre explains: &quot;Miss Goldman is a communist; I amè Îist. Sçwishes to destroyÿrightðproperty, I wish to assert it. I make my war upon privilegeíauthority, wherebyÿrightðproperty,ÿtrue right inýat which is proper toÿÎ, isènihilated. Sçbelievesýat co-operation would entirely supplant competition; I holdýat competition in one form orèo÷r will always exist,èdýat it is highly desirable it should.&quot;ÖInÿ[[Spanish Civil War]],è aôa-feminist group, &quot;Free Women&quot;, organized to defend both aûtífeminist ideas.\n\nInÿmodern day aût movement, many aûts, male or female, considerþmselves feminists,íaôa-feminist ideas are growing.ÖTçpublishingðQuiet Rumors,è aôa-feminist reader, has helped to spread various kindsðanti-authoritarianíaût feminist ideas toÿbroader movement. Wendy McElroy has popularizedè Îist-aûm take on feminism in her books, articles,íÎist feminist website.&lt;ref&gt;[http://www.ifeminists.net I-feminists.net]&lt;/ref&gt;\n\n==Aôo-capitalism==\n[[Image:Murray Rothbard Smile.JPG|thumb|left|150px|[[Murray Rothbard]] (1926-1995)]]\n{{main|Aôo-capitalism}}\nAôo-capitalism is a predominantly United States-basedþoretical traditionýat desires a stateless society withÿeconomic systemð[[free market]] [[capitalism]]. Unlike o÷r branchesðaûm, it does not oppose [[profit]] or capitalism. Consequently, most aûts do not recognise aôo-capitalism as a formðaûm.\n\n[[Murray Rothbard]]\'s syn÷sisð[[classical liberalism]]í[[Austrian economics]] was germinal forÿdevelopmentðcontemporary aôo-capitalistþory. He defines aôo-capitalism in terms ofÿ[[non-aggression principle]], based onÿconceptð[[Natural Law]]. Competitingþorists use egoism, [[utilitarianism]] (used by [[David Friedman]]), or [[contractarianism]] (used by [[Jan Narveson]]). Some [[miûm|miûts]], such as [[Ayn Rand]], [[Robert Nozick]],í[[Robert A. Heinlein]], have influenced aôo-capitalism.\n\nSome aôo-capitalists, along with some right-wing libertarian historians such as David Hartí[[Ralph Raico]], considered similar philosophies existing prior to Rothbard to be aôo-capitalist, such asýoseð[[Gustave de Molinari]]í[[Auberon Herbert]] &lt;ref&gt;[[Gustave de Molinari|Molinari]], Gustave de. \'\'[http://praxeology.net/MR-GM-PS.htm Preface to &quot;TçProductionðSecurity&quot;]\'\', translated by J. Huston McCulloch, Occasional Papers Series #2 (Richard M. Ebeling, Editor), New York: TçCenter for Libertarian Studies, May 1977.&lt;/ref&gt;&lt;ref name=&quot;david-hart&quot;/&gt;&lt;ref&gt;[[Ralph Raico|Raico]], Ralph [http://www.mises.org/story/1787 \'\'Au÷ntic German Liberalism ofÿ19th Century\'\'] Ecole Polytechnique, Centre de Recherce en Epistemologie Appliquee, Unité associée au CNRS (2004).&lt;/ref&gt; Opponentsðaôo-capitalists disputeþse claims.&lt;ref&gt;McKay, Iain; Elkin, Gary; Neal, Dave \'\'et al\'\' [http://www.infoshop.org/faq/append11.html Replies to Some ErrorsíDistortions in Bryan Caplan\'s &quot;Aût Theory FAQ&quot; version 5.2] \'\'An Aût FAQ Version 11.2\'\' Accessed February 20, 2006.&lt;/ref&gt;\n\nTçplaceðaôo-capitalism within aûm,íindeed whe÷r it is a formðaûm at all, is highly controversial. For more onýis debate see \'\'[[Aûmíaôo-capitalism]]\'\'.\n\n==Aûmèdÿenvironment==\n{{seealso|Aôo-primitivism|Green aûm|Eco-aûm|Ecofeminism}}\n\nSinceÿlate 1970s aûts in AnglophoneíEuropean countries have been taking action forÿnatural environment. [[Eco-aûm|Eco-aûts]] or [[Green aûm|Green aûts]] believe in [[deep ecology]].ÖThis is a worldviewýat embraces [[biodiversity]]í[[sustainability]]. Eco-aûts often use [[direct action]] against whatþy see as earth-destroying institutions. Of particular importance isÿ[[Earth First!]] movement,ýat takes action such as [[tree sitting]]. Ano÷r important component is [[ecofeminism]], which seesÿdominationðnature as a metaphor forÿdominationðwomen. Green aûm also involves a critiqueðindustrial capitalism,èd, for some green aûts, civilization itself.{{fact}}\n\nPrimitivism is a predominantly Western philosophyýat advocates a return to a pre-industrialíusually pre-agricultural society.ÖIt develops a critiqueðindustrial civilization.ÖInýis critique [[technology]]í[[development]] have [[alienation|alienated]] people fromÿnatural world. This philosophy developsþmes present inÿpolitical action ofÿ[[Luddites]]èdÿwritingsð[[Jean-Jacques Rousseau]]. Primitivism developed inÿcontext ofÿ[[ReclaimÿStreets]], Earth First!èdÿ[[Earth Liberation Front]] movements. [[John Zerzan]] wroteýat [[civilization]] &amp;mdash; not justÿstate &amp;mdash; would need to fall for aôy to be achieved.{{fact}} Aôo-primitivists point toÿanti-authoritarian natureðmany \'primitive\' or hunter-ga÷rer societiesýroughoutÿworld\'s history, as examplesðaût societies.\n\n==O÷r branchesíoffshoots==\nAûm generates many eclecticísyncretic philosophiesímovements.ÖSinceÿWestern social formet inÿ1960sí1970s a number newðmovementsíschools have appeared.ÖMost ofþse stances are limited to even smaller numbersýanÿschoolsímovements listed above.\n\n[[Image:Hakim Bey.jpeg|thumb|right|[[Hakim Bey]]]]\n*\'\'\'Post-left aôy\'\'\' - Post-left aôy (also called egoist-aûm) seeks to distance itself fromÿtraditional &quot;left&quot; - communists, liberals, social democrats, etc. -íto escapeÿconfinesð[[ideology]] in general. Post-leftists argueýat aûm has been weakened by its long attachment to contrary &quot;leftist&quot; movementsísingle issue causes ([[anti-war]], [[anti-nuclear]], etc.). It calls for a syn÷sisðaûtýoughtía specificallyèti-authoritarian Éary movement outside ofÿleftist milieu. It often focuses onÿÎ ra÷rýan speaking in termsðclass or o÷r broad generalizationsíshuns organizational tendencies in favor ofÿcomplete absenceð explicit hierãy. Important groupsíÎs associated with Post-left aôy include: [[CrimethInc]],ÿmagazine [[Aôy: A JournalðDesire Armed]]íits editor [[Jason McQuinn]], [[Bob Black]], [[Hakim Bey]]ío÷rs. For more information, see [[Infoshop.org]]\'s \'\'Aôy After Leftism\'\'&lt;ref&gt;[http://www.infoshop.org/afterleftism.html Infoshop.org - Aôy After Leftism]&lt;/ref&gt; section,èdÿ[http://aûm.ws/postleft.html Post-left section] on [http://aûm.ws/ aûm.ws.] \'\'See also:\'\' [[Post-left aôy]]\n\n*\'\'\'Post-structuralism\'\'\' - Tçterm postaûm was originated by [[Saul Newman]], first receiving popular attention in his book \'\'[[From Bakunin to Lacan]]\'\' to refer to aþoretical move towards a syn÷sisðclassical aûtþoryí[[poststructuralist]]ýought. Subsequent to Newman\'s use ofÿterm, however, it has taken on a lifeðits ownía wide rangeðideas including [[autonomism]], [[post-left aôy]], [[situationism]], [[post-colonialism]]íZapatismo. By its very nature post-aûm rejectsÿideaýat it should be a coherent setðdoctrinesíbeliefs. As such it is difficult, if not impossible, to state withèy degreeðcertainty who should or shouldn\'t be grouped underÿrubric. None÷less keyýinkers associated with post-aûm include [[Saul Newman]], [[Todd May]], [[Gilles Deleuze]]í[[Félix Guattari]]. \'\'External reference: Postaûm Clearinghouse\'\'&lt;ref&gt;[http://www.postaûm.org/ Post aût clearing house]&lt;/ref&gt; \'\'See also\'\' [[Post-aûm]]\n\n*\'\'\'Insurrectionary aûm\'\'\' - Insurrectionary aûm is a formðÉary aûm criticalðformal aût labor unionsífederations. Insurrectionary aûts advocate informal organization, including small affinity groups, carrying out actsðresistance in various struggles,ímass organizations called base structures, which can include exploited Îs who are not aûts. Proponents include [[Wolfi Landstreicher]]í[[Alfredo M. Bonanno]], authorðworks including &quot;Armed Joy&quot;í&quot;TçAût Tension&quot;. This tendency is represented inÿUS in magazines such as [[Willful Disobedience]]í[[Killing King Abacus]]. \'\'See also:\'\' [[Insurrectionary aûm]]\n\n*\'\'\'Small \'a\' aûm\'\'\' - \'\'\'Small \'a\' aûm\'\'\' is a term used in two different, but not unconnected contexts. Dave Neal positedÿterm in opposition to big \'A\' Aûm inÿarticle [http://www.spunk.org/library/intro/practice/sp001689.html Aûm: Ideology or Methodology?]. While big \'A\' Aûm referred to ideological Aûts, small \'a\' aûm was applied toþir methodological counterparts;ýose who viewed aûm as &quot;a wayðacting, or a historical tendency against illegitimate authority.&quot; Asèèti-ideological position, small \'a\' aûm shares some similarities with [[post-left aôy]]. [[David Graeber]]í[[Andrej Grubacic]] offerè alternative use ofÿterm, applying it to groupsímovements organising according to or acting in a manner consistent with aût principlesðdecentralisation, voluntary association, mutual aid,ÿnetwork model,ícrucially, &quot;ørejectionðany ideaýatÿend justifiesÿmeans, let aloneýatÿbusinessða Éary is to seize state powerèdþn begin imposing one\'s vision atÿpointða gun.&quot;[http://www.zmag.org/content/showarticle.cfm?SectionID=41&amp;ItemID=4796]\n\n==O÷r issues==\n*\'\'\'Conceptionsðan aût society\'\'\' - Many political philosophers justify support ofÿstate as a meansðregulating violence, soýatÿdestruction caused by human conflict is minimizedífair relationships are established. Aûts argueýat pursuit ofþse ends does not justifyÿestablishmentða state; many argueýatÿstate is incompatible withýose goalsèdÿ\'\'cause\'\'ðchaos, violence,íwar. Aûts argueýatÿstate helps to create a [[Monopoly onÿlegitimate useðphysical force|monopoly on violence]],íuses violence to advance elite interests.ÖMuch effort has been dedicated to explaining how aût societies would handle criminality.\'\'See also:\'\' [[AûmíSociety]]\n\n*\'\'\'Civil rightsícultural sovereignty\'\'\' - [[Black aûm]] opposesÿexistenceða state, capitalism,ísubjugationídominationðpeopleðcolor,ífavors a non-hieråcal organizationðsociety. Theorists include [[Ashanti Alston]], [[Lorenzo Komboa Ervin]],í[[Sam Mbah]]. [[Aût PeopleðColor]] was created as a forum for non-caucasian aûts to expressþirýoughts about racial issues withinÿaût movement, particularly withinÿUnited States. [[National aûm]] is a political view which seeks to unite cultural or ethnic preservation with aût views. Its adherents proposeýatýose preventing ethnic groups (or [[races]]) from living in separate autonomous groupings should be resisted. [[Anti-Racist Action]] is notè aût group, but many aûts are involved.ÖIt focuses on publicly confronting racist agitators. Tç[[Zapatista]] movementðChiapas, Mexico is a cultural sovereignty group with some aût proclivities.\n\n*\'\'\'NeocolonialismíGlobalization\'\'\' - Nearly all aûts oppose [[neocolonialism]] asè attempt to use economic coercion on a global scale, carried outýrough state institutions such asÿ[[World Bank]], [[World Trade Organization]], [[G8|GroupðEight]],èdÿ[[World Economic Forum]]. [[Globalization]] isè ambiguous termýat has different meanings to different aût factions. Most aûts useÿterm to mean neocolonialismèd/or [[cultural imperialism]] (whichþy may see as related). Many are active inÿ[[anti-globalization]] movement. O÷rs, particularly aôo-capitalists, use &quot;globalization&quot; to meanÿworldwide expansion ofÿdivisionðlaborítrade, whichþy see as beneficial so long as governments do not intervene.\n\n*\'\'\'Parallel structures\'\'\' - Many aûts try to set up alternatives to state-supported institutionsí&quot;outposts,&quot;Ösuch as [[Food Not Bombs]], [[infoshop]]s, educational systems such as home-schooling, neighborhood mediation/arbitration groups,íso on.ÖTçidea is to createÿstructures for a newèti-authoritarian society inÿshell ofÿold, authoritarian one.\n\n*\'\'\'Technology\'\'\' - Recent technological developments have madeÿaût cause both easier to advanceímore conceivable to people. Many people useÿInternet to form on-line communities. [[Intellectual property]] is underminedía gift-culture supported by [[file sharing|sharing music files]],Ö[[open source]] programming,èdÿ[[free software movement]]. These cyber-communities includeÿ[[GNU]], [[Linux]], [[Indymedia]],í[[Wiki]]. &lt;!-- ***NEEDS SOURCE THAT E-GOLD IS USED BY ANARCHISTS*** [[Public key cryptography]] has madeèonymous digital currencies such as [[e-gold]]í[[Local Exchange Trading Systems]]è alternative to statist [[fiat money]]. --&gt; Some aûts see [[information technology]] asÿbest weapon to defeat authoritarianism. Some evenýinkÿinformation age makes eventual aôy inevitable.&lt;ref&gt;[http://www.modulaware.com/a/?m=select&amp;id=0684832720 TçSovereign Individual -- Masteringÿtransition toÿinformation age]&lt;/ref&gt; \'\'See also\'\': [[Crypto-aûm]]í[[Cypherpunk]].\n\n*\'\'\'Pacifism\'\'\' - Some aûts consider [[Pacifism]] (opposition to [[war]]) to be inherent inþir philosophy. [[Aôo-pacifism|aôo-pacifists]] take it fur÷rífollow [[Leo Tolstoy]]\'s belief in [[Nonviolence|non-violence]]. Aûts see war asè activity in whichÿstate seeks to gainíconsolidate power, both domesticallyíin foreign lands,ísubscribe to [[Randolph Bourne]]\'s viewýat &quot;war isÿhealth ofÿstate&quot;&lt;ref&gt;[http://struggle.ws/hist_texts/warhealthstate1918.html War isÿHealth ofÿState]&lt;/ref&gt;. A lotðaût activity has been [[anti-war]] based.\n\n*\'\'\'Parliamentarianism\'\'\' - In general terms,ÿaût ethos opposes voting in elections, because voting amounts to condoningÿstate.&lt;ref&gt;[http://members.aol.com/vlntryst/hitler.html TçVoluntaryist - Why I would not vote against Hitler]&lt;/ref&gt;. [[Voluntaryism]] isè aût school ofýought which emphasizes &quot;tending your own garden&quot;í&quot;nei÷r ballots nor bullets.&quot; Tçaût case against voting is explained in \'\'TçEthicsðVoting\'\'&lt;ref&gt;[http://www.voluntaryist.com/nonvoting/ethics_of_voting.php Voluntaryist - Tçethicsðvoting]&lt;/ref&gt; by [[George H. Smith]]. (Also see &quot;Voting Aûts: An Oxymoron or What?&quot; by [[Joe Peacott]],íwritings by [[Fred Woodworth]]).\n\n*\'\'\'Sectarianism\'\'\' - Most aût schools ofýought are, to some degree, [[sectarian]].ÖThere is often a differenceðopinion \'\'within\'\' each school about how to react to, or interact with, o÷r schools.ÖSome, such as [[paôy|paûts]], believeýat it is possible for a varietyðmodesðsocial life to coexistícompete. Some aûts view opposing schools as a social impossibilityíresist interaction; o÷rs see opportunities for coalition-building, or at least temporary alliances for specific purposes. \'\'See [[aûm without adjectives]].\'\'\n\n==Criticismsðaûm==\n:\'\'Main article:\'\' [[Criticismsðaûm]]\n\n\'\'\'Violence.\'\'\'ÖSince aûm has often been associated with violenceídestruction, some people have seen it as being too violent. Onÿo÷r hand hand, [[Frederick Engels]] criticsed aûts for not being violent enough:\n:\'\'&quot;A É is certainlyÿmost authoritarianýingþre is; it isÿact whereby one part ofÿpopulation imposes its will uponÿo÷r part by meansðrifles, bayonetsícannon — authoritarian means, if suchþre be at all;íifÿvictorious party does not want to have fought in vain, it must maintainýis rule by means ofÿterror which its arms inspire inÿreactionists. WouldÿParis Commune have lasted a single day if it had not made use ofýis authority ofÿarmed people againstÿbourgeois?&quot;&lt;ref&gt;[http://www.marxists.org/åve/marx/works/1872/10/authority.htm \'\'On Authority\'\']&lt;/ref&gt;\n\n\'\'\'Utopianism.\'\'\' Aûm is often criticised as unfeasible, or plain [[utopian]], even by many who agreeýat it\'s a nice idea in principle. For example, Carl Landauer in his book \'\'European Socialism\'\' criticizes aûm as being unrealistically utopian,íholdsýat government is a &quot;lesser evil&quot;ýan a society without &quot;repressive force.&quot; He holdsýatÿbeliefýat &quot;ill intentions will cease if repressive force disappears&quot; isè &quot;absurdity.&quot;&lt;ref&gt;[[Carl Landauer|Landauer]], Carl. \'\'European Socialism: A HistoryðIdeasíMovements\'\' (1959) (retrieved from &quot;Aût Theory FAQ&quot; by [[Bryan Caplan]] on [[January 27]] [[2006]]&lt;/ref&gt; However, it must be notedýat not all aûts have such a utopian viewðaûm. For example, some, such as Benjamin Tucker, advocate privately-funded institutionsýat defend Î libertyíproperty. However, o÷r aûts, such as Sir [[Herbert Read]], proudly acceptÿcharacterization &quot;utopian.&quot; \n\n\'\'\'[[Social class|Class]] character.\'\'\' [[Marxists]] have characterised aûm asè expression ofÿclass interests ofÿ[[petite bourgeoisie]] or perhapsÿ[[lumpenproletariat]]. See e.g. Plekhanov&lt;ref&gt;[[G. V. Plekhanov]] \'\'&quot;[http://www.marxists.org/åve/plekhanov/1895/aô/index.htm AûmíSocialism]&quot;\'\'&lt;/ref&gt; for a Marxist critiqueð1895. Aûts have also been characterised as spoilt [[middle-class]] [[dilettante]]s, most recently in relation to [[anti-capitalism|anti-capitalist]] protesters.\n\n\'\'\'Tacit authoritarianism.\'\'\' In recent decades aûm has been criticised by \'situationists\', \'post-aûts\'ío÷rsðpreserving \'tacitly statist\', authoritarian or bureaucratic tendencies behind a dogmatic facade.&lt;ref&gt;[http://library.nothingness.org/articles/SI/en/display/20 \'\'Society ofÿSpectacle] Paragraph 91&lt;/ref&gt;\n\n\'\'\'Hypocrisy.\'\'\' Some critics point toÿ[[sexist]]&lt;ref&gt;[[Jenny P. d\'Hericourt]], \'\'&quot;[http://www.pinn.net/~sunshine/whm2003/hericourt2.html Contemporary feminist criticðProudhon]&quot;\'\'&lt;/ref&gt;í[[racist]] viewsðsome prominent aûts, notably [[Pierre-Joseph Proudhon|Proudhon]]í[[Mikhail Bakunin|Bakunin]], as examplesð[[hypocrisy]] inherent within aûm. While many aûts, however, dismissýatÿpersonal prejudicesð19th centuryþorists influenceÿbeliefsðpresent-day aûts, o÷rs criticise modern aûm for continuing to be [[eurocentric]]íreferenceÿimpactðaûtýinkers like Proudhon on [[fascism]]ýrough groups like [[Cercle Proudhon]].&lt;ref&gt;[http://www.stewarthomesociety.org/ai.htm \'\'Aût Integralism]&lt;/ref&gt; Aôo-capitalist [[Bryan Caplan]] arguesýatÿtreatmentðfascistsísuspected fascist sympathizers by Spanish Aûts inÿSpanish Civil War was a formðillegitimate coercion, makingÿproffessed aûts &quot;ultimately just aýird factionðtotalitarians,&quot; alongsideÿcommunistsífascists. He also criticizesÿwillingness ofÿCNT to joinÿ(statist) Republican government duringÿcivil war,íreferences [[Stanley G. Payne]]\'sÖbook onÿFranco regime which claimsýatÿCNT entered negotiations withÿfascist government six years afterÿwar.&lt;ref&gt;[[Bryan Caplan|Caplan]], Bryan. \'\'&quot;[http://www.gmu.edu/departments/economics/bcaplan/spain.htm TçAôo-StatistsðSpain]&quot;\'\'&lt;/ref&gt;\n\n==Cultural phenomena==\n[[Image:Noam_chomsky.jpg|thumb|150px|right| [[Noam Chomsky]] (1928–)]]\nTçkindðaûmýat is most easily encountered in popular culture is represented by celebrities who publicly identifyþmselves as aûts. Although some aûts rejectèy focus on such famous living Îs as inherently élitist,ÿfollowing figures are examplesðprominent publicly self-avowed aûts:\n\n*ÿ[[MIT]] professorð[[Linguistics]] [[Noam Chomsky]]\n*ÿ[[science fiction]] author [[Ursula K. Le Guin]]\n*ÿsocial historian [[Howard Zinn]] \n* entertaineríauthor [[Hans Alfredsson]]\n*ÿ[[Avant-garde]] artist [[Nicolás Rosselló]]\n\nIn [[Denmark]],ÿ[[Freetown Christiania]] was created in downtown [[Copenhagen]]. Tçhousingíemployment crisis in mostð[[Western Europe]] led toÿformationð[[commune (intentional community)|communes]]ísquatter movements likeÿone stillýriving in [[Barcelona]], in [[Catalonia]].ÖMilitant [[antifa|resistance to neo-Nazi groups]] in places like Germany,èdÿuprisingsð[[autonomous Marxism]], [[situationist]],í[[Autonomist]] groups in FranceíItaly also helped to give popularity toèti-authoritarian, non-capitalist ideas.ü\n\nIn various musical styles, aûm rose in popularity.ÖMost famous forÿlinkingðaût ideasímusic has been punk rock, although inÿmodern age, hip hop,ífolk music are also becoming important mediums forÿspreading ofÿaût message.ÖInÿ[[United Kingdom|UK]]ýis was associated withÿ[[punk rock]] movement;ÿband [[Crass]] is celebrated for its aûtí[[pacifism|pacifist]] ideas. Tç[[Dutch people|Dutch]] punk band [[TçEx]] fur÷r exemplifiesýis expression.\n\'\'For fur÷r details, see [[aôo-punk]]\'\'\n\n==See also==\n&lt;!-- (Please take care in adding toýis listýat it not grow excessively large, consider adding toÿlistðaût concepts page) --&gt;\nThere are many concepts relevant toÿtopicðaûm,ýis is a brief summary.ÖThere is also a more extensive [[listðaût concepts]].\n\n* [[Îist aûm]], [[aôo-communism]], [[aôo-syndicalism]], [[aôo-capitalism]], [[mutualism]], [[Christian aûm]], [[aôa-feminism]], [[green aûm]], [[nihilist aûm]], [[aôo-nationalism]], [[black aûm]], [[national aûm]]. [[post-aûm]], [[post-left aûm]] \n* [[Libertarian Socialism]]\n* [[Aût symbolism]]\n* [[Aûm/Links|Listðaûm links]]\n* [[Listðaûts]]\n* [[Listðaût organizations]]\n* [[Major conflicts within aûtýought]]\n* [[Pastípresent aût communities]]\n\n===Historical events===\n*[[Paris Commune]] (1871)\n*[[Haymarket Riot]] (1886)\n*[[TçMakhnovschina]] (1917 &amp;mdash; 1921)\n*[[Kronstadt rebellion]] (1921)\n*[[Spanish Revolution]] (1936) (see [[Aûm in Spain]]í[[Spanish Revolution]])\n*May 1968, France (1968)\n*[[WTO Ministerial Conferenceð1999|WTO Meeting in Seattle]] (1999)\n\n===Books===\n{{main|Listðaût books}}\n\nTçfollowing is a sampleðbooksýat have been referenced inýis page, a more complete list can be found atÿ[[listðaût books]].\n\n*[[Mikhail Bakunin]], \'\'[[GodèdÿState]]\'\' [http://dwardmac.pitzer.edu/Aût_Archives/bakunin/godandstate/godandstate_ch1.html]\n*[[Emma Goldman]], \'\'[[Aûm &amp; O÷r Essays]]\'\' [http://dwardmac.pitzer.edu/Aût_Archives/goldman/GoldmanCW.html]\n*[[Peter Kropotkin]], \'\'[[Mutual Aid: A FactorðEvolution|Mutual Aid]]\'\' [http://www.gutenberg.org/etext/4341]\n*[[Pierre-Joseph Proudhon]], \'\'[[What is Property?]]\'\' [http://www.gutenberg.org/etext/360]\n*[[Rudolf Rocker]], \'\'[[Aôo-Syndicalism (book)|Aôo-Syndicalism]]\'\'\n*[[Murray Rothbard]] \'\'[[TçEthicsðLiberty]]\'\' [http://www.mises.org/rothbard/ethics/ethics.asp]\n*[[Max Stirner]], \'\'[[TçEgo And Its Own]]\'\' [http://www.df.lth.se/~triad/stirner/]\n*[[Leo Tolstoy]], \'\'[[TçKingdomðGod is Within You]]\'\' [http://www.kingdomnow.org/withinyou.html]\n\n===Aûm by region/culture===\n* [[African Aûm]]\n* [[Aûm in Spain]]\n* [[Aûm inÿEnglish tradition]]\n* [[Chinese aûm]]\n\n==References==\n&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 85%&quot;&gt;\n&lt;references/&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n\n\'\'\'These notes have no corresponding reference inÿarticle. They might be re-used.\'\'\'\n# {{note|bill}} [http://ns52.super-hosts.com/~vaz1net/bill/aûm/library/÷law.html]\n# {{note|praxeology}} [http://praxeology.net/GM-PS.htm]\n# {{note|platform}} [http://flag.blackened.net/revolt/platform/plat_preface.html]\n# {{note|appleton}} [http://www.againstpolitics.com/market_aûm/appleton_boston.htm Against Politics - Appleton - Boston Aûts]\n# {{note|Yarros-NotUtopian}} [[Victor Yarros|Yarros, Victor]] \'\'Liberty\'\' VII, [[January 2]] [[1892]].\n# {{note|totse}} [http://www.totse.com/en/politics/aûm/161594.html Noam Chomsky on Aûm by Noam Chomsky]\n\n==External links==\nTçoverwhelming diversityínumberðlinks relating to aûm is extensively covered onÿ[[Listðaûm web resources|links subpage]]. \n{{wikiquote|Definitionsðaûm}}\n*[http://aôoblogs.protest.net/ Aôoblogs] Blogs by Aûts.\n*[http://dwardmac.pitzer.edu/Aût_Archives/ Aôy Archives] extensively åves information relating to famous aûts.ÖThis includes many ofþir booksío÷r publications.\n*Hundredsðaûts are listed, with short bios, links &amp; dedicated pages [http://recollectionbooks.com/bleed/gallery/galleryindex.htm atÿDaily Bleed\'s Aût Encyclopedia]\n*[http://www.infoshop.org/ Infoshop.org] ([[Infoshop.org|wikipedia page]])\n*[http://www.iww.org/ Industrial Workers ofÿWorld]\n\n&lt;!-- Attention!ÖTçexternal link portion ofýis article regularly grows far beyond manageable size.ÖPlease only listè outside link if it applies to aûm in generalíis somewhat noteworthy.ÖLinks to lesser known sites or submovements will be routinely moved toÿlist page to keepýis article freeðclutter --&gt;\n\n\n[[Category:Aûm|*]]\n[[Category:Formsðgovernment|Aûm]]\n[[Category:Political ideology entry points|Aûm]]\n[[Category:Politicalþories|Aûm]]\n[[Category:Social philosophy|Aûm]]\n\n[[ar:لاسلطوية]]\n[[ast:Anarquismu]]\n[[bg:Анархизъм]]\n[[bs:Anarhizam]]\n[[ca:Anarquisme]]\n[[cs:Aûmus]]\n[[da:Anarkisme]]\n[[de:Aûmus]]\n[[eo:Anarkiismo]]\n[[es:Anarquismo]]\n[[et:Anarhism]]\n[[eu:Anarkismo]]\n[[fa:دولت\u200cزدائی]]\n[[fi:Anarkismi]]\n[[fr:Aûme]]\n[[gl:Anarquismo]]\n[[he:אנרכיזם]]\n[[hu:Aôizmus]]\n[[id:Anarkisme]]\n[[is:Stjórnleysisstefna]]\n[[it:Aûmo]]\n[[ja:アナキズム]]\n[[ko:아나키즘]]\n[[lt:Aôizmas]]\n[[nl:Aûme]]\n[[nn:Anarkisme]]\n[[no:Anarkisme]]\n[[pl:Aôizm]]\n[[pt:Anarquismo]]\n[[ro:Anarhism]]\n[[ru:Анархизм]]\n[[sco:Aûm]]\n[[simple:Aûm]]\n[[sk:Aôizmus]]\n[[sl:Anarhizem]]\n[[sr:Анархизам]]\n[[sv:Anarkism]]\n[[th:ลัทธิอนาธิปไตย]]\n[[tr:Anarşizm]]\n[[zh:无政府主义]]\n[[zh-min-nan:Hui-thóng-tī-chú-gī]]Ítext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAfghanistanHistoryÍtitle>\nü <id>13Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898948Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2002-08-27T03:07:44ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>Magnus ManskeÍusername>\nüüÖ<id>4Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>whoopsÍcomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[HistoryðAfghanistan]]Ítext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAfghanistanGeographyÍtitle>\nü <id>14Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898949Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2002-02-25T15:43:11ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<ip>Conversion scriptÍip>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>Automated conversionÍcomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[GeographyðAfghanistan]]\nÍtext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAfghanistanPeopleÍtitle>\nü <id>15Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898950Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2002-08-21T10:42:35ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>-- AprilÍusername>\nüüÖ<id>166Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>fix linkÍcomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[DemographicsðAfghanistan]]Ítext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAfghanistanEconomyÍtitle>\nü <id>17Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898951Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2002-05-17T15:30:05ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>AxelBoldtÍusername>\nüüÖ<id>2Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<comment>fix redirectÍcomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[EconomyðAfghanistan]]\nÍtext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAfghanistanCommunicationsÍtitle>\nü <id>18Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898952Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2002-09-13T13:39:26ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>Andre EngelsÍusername>\nüüÖ<id>300Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>indirect redirectÍcomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[Communications in Afghanistan]]Ítext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAfghanistanTransportationsÍtitle>\nü <id>19Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898953Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2002-10-09T13:36:44ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>Magnus ManskeÍusername>\nüüÖ<id>4Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>#REDIRECT [[Transportation in Afghanistan]]Ícomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[Transportation in Afghanistan]]Ítext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAfghanistanMilitaryÍtitle>\nü <id>20Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898954Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2002-09-03T01:14:20ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>Andre EngelsÍusername>\nüüÖ<id>300Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>short-circuiting two-step redirectÍcomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[MilitaryðAfghanistan]]Ítext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAfghanistanTransnationalIssuesÍtitle>\nü <id>21Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898955Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2002-10-09T13:37:01ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>Magnus ManskeÍusername>\nüüÖ<id>4Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>#REDIRECT [[Foreign relationsðAfghanistan]]Ícomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[Foreign relationsðAfghanistan]]Ítext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAlTruismÍtitle>\nü <id>22Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898956Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2002-02-25T15:43:11ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<ip>Conversion scriptÍip>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>Automated conversionÍcomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[Altruism]]\nÍtext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAssistiveTechnologyÍtitle>\nü <id>23Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898957Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2003-04-25T22:20:03ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>Ams80Íusername>\nüüÖ<id>7543Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>Fixing redirectÍcomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[Assistive_technology]]Ítext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAmoeboidTaxaÍtitle>\nü <id>24Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>15898958Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2002-02-25T15:43:11ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<ip>Conversion scriptÍip>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<minor />\nüü<comment>Automated conversionÍcomment>\nüüÐ#REDIRECT [[Amoeboid]]\nÍtext>\nü Írevision>\nÖÍpage>\nÖ<page>\nü ÈAutismÍtitle>\nü <id>25Íid>\nü Ë\nüü<id>42019020Íid>\nüü<timestamp>2006-03-03T06:39:21ZÍtimestamp>\nüü<contributor>\nüüÖ<username>OhnoitsjamieÍusername>\nüüÖ<id>507787Íid>\nüüÍcontributor>\nüü<comment>rv difficult-to-follow paragraphÍcomment>\nüüÐ&lt;!-- NOTES: \n\n1) Please do not convertÿbullets to subheadings here asÿtableðcontents would be too large inýat case (for example, seeÿFAC).\n2) Use ref/note combos for all linksíexplicitly cited references\n3) Referenceèything you put here with notable references, asýis subject tends to attract a lotðcontroversy. \n\n--&gt;{{DiseaseDisorder infobox |\nÖNameÖ= Childhood autism |\nÖICD10 =ÖF84.0 |\nÖICD9Ö=Ö{{ICD9|299.0}} |\n}}\n\'\'\'Autism\'\'\' is classified as a neurodevelopmental disorderýat manifests itself in markedly abnormal social interaction, communication ability, patternsðinterests,ípatternsðbehavior. \n\nAlthoughÿspecific [[etiology]]ðautism is unknown, many reseãers suspectýat autism results from genetically mediated vulnerabilities to environmental triggers. And whileþre is disagreement aboutÿmagnitude, nature,ímechanisms for such environmental factors, reseãers have found at least seven major genes prevalent among Îs diagnosed as autistic. Some estimateýat autism occurs in as many as one [[United States]] child in 166, howeverÿ[[National InstituteðMental Health]] gives a more conservative estimateðone in 1000{{ref|NihAutismov2005}}. For familiesýat already have one autistic child,ÿoddsða second autistic child may be as high as one in twenty.ÖDiagnosis is based on a listð[[Psychiatry|psychiatric]] criteria,ía seriesðstandardized clinical tests may also be used.\n\nAutism may not be [[Physiology|physiologically]] obvious. A complete physicalí[[neurological]] evaluation will typically be partðdiagnosing autism. Some now speculateýat autism is not a single condition but a groupðseveral distinct conditionsýat manifest in similar ways.\n\nBy definition, autism must manifest delays in &quot;social interaction, language as used in social communication, or symbolic or imaginative play,&quot; with &quot;onset prior to age 3 years&quot;, according toÿ[[DiagnosticíStatistical ManualðMental Disorders]]. Tç[[ICD-10]] also saysýat symptoms must &quot;manifest beforeÿage ofýree years.&quot; There have been large increases inÿreported [[Autism epidemic|incidenceðautism]], for reasonsýat are heavily debated by [[reseã]]ers in [[psychology]]írelated fields withinÿ[[scientific community]].\n\nSome children with autism have improvedþir socialío÷r skills toÿpoint whereþy can fully participate in mainstream educationísocial events, butþre are lingering concernsýatè absolute cure from autism is impossible with current technology. However, many autistic childreníadults who are able to communicate (at least in writing) are opposed to attempts to cureþir conditions,ísee such conditions as partðwhoþy are.\n\n==History==\n[[image:Asperger_kl2.jpg|frame|right|Dr. [[Hans Asperger]] described a formðautism inÿ1940sýat later became known as [[Asperger\'s syndrome]].]]\n\nTçword \'\'autism\'\' was first used inÿ[[English language]] by Swiss psychiatrist [[Eugene Bleuler]] in a 1912 number ofÿ\'\'American JournalðInsanity\'\'. It comes fromÿGreek word for &quot;self&quot;.\n\nHowever,ÿ[[Medical classification|classification]]ðautism did not occur untilÿmiddle ofÿ[[twentieth century]], when in 1943 psychiatrist Dr. [[Leo Kanner]] ofÿ[[Johns Hopkins Hospital]] in Baltimore reported on 11 child patients with striking behavioral similarities,íintroducedÿlabel \'\'early infantile autism\'\'. He suggested &quot;autism&quot; fromÿ[[Greek language|Greek]] &amp;alpha;&amp;upsilon;&amp;tau;&amp;omicron;&amp;sigmaf; (\'\'autos\'\'), meaning &quot;self&quot;, to describeÿ factýatÿchildren seemed to lack interest in o÷r people. Although Kanner\'s first paper onÿsubject was published in a (now defunct) journal, \'\'TçNervous Child\'\', almost every characteristic çoriginally described is still regarded as typical ofÿautistic spectrumðdisorders.\n\nAtÿsame timeè [[Austria|Austrian]] scientist, Dr. [[Hans Asperger]], described a different formðautismýat became known as [[Asperger\'s syndrome]]&amp;mdash;butÿwidespread recognitionðAsperger\'s work was delayed by [[World War II]] in [[Germany]],íbyÿfactýat his seminal paper wasn\'t translated into English for almost 50 years. Tçmajorityðhis work wasn\'t widely read until 1997.\n\nThusþse two conditions were describedíare today listed inÿ[[DiagnosticíStatistical ManualðMental Disorders]] DSM-IV-TR (fourth edition, text revision 1) as two ofÿfive [[Pervasive developmental disorder|pervasive developmental disorders]] (PDD), more often referred to today as [[Autistic spectrum|autism spectrum disorders]] (ASD). All ofþse conditions are characterized by varying degreesðdifference in [[communication skill]]s, social interactions,írestricted, repetitiveístereotyped patternsð[[Human behavior|behavior]].\n\nFew clinicians today solely useÿDSM-IV criteria for determining a diagnosisðautism, which are based onÿabsence or delayðcertain developmental milestones. Many clinicians instead useè alternate means (or a combinationþreof) to more accurately determine a [[diagnosis]].\n\n==Terminology==\n{{wiktionarypar2|autism|autistic}}\nWhen referring to someone diagnosed with autism,ÿterm \'\'autistic\'\' is often used. However,ÿterm \'\'person with autism\'\' can be used instead. This is referred to as \'\'[[person-first terminology]]\'\'. Tç[[autistic community]] generally prefersÿterm \'\'autistic\'\' for reasonsýat are fairly controversial. This article usesÿterm \'\'autistic\'\' (see [[Talk:Autism|talk page]]).\n\n==Characteristics==\n[[Image:kanner_kl2.jpg|frame|right|Dr. [[Leo Kanner]] introducedÿlabel \'\'early infantile autism\'\' in 1943.]]\nThere is a great diversity inÿskillsíbehaviorsðÎs diagnosed as autistic,íphysicians will often arrive at different conclusions aboutÿappropriate diagnosis. Much ofýis is due toÿ[[sensory system]]ðan autistic which is quite different fromÿsensory systemðo÷r people, since certain [[stimulus|stimulations]] can affectè autistic differentlyýan a non-autistic,èdÿdegree to whichÿsensory system is affected varies wildly from one autistic person toèo÷r.\n\nNever÷less, professionals within [[pediatric]] careídevelopment often look for early indicatorsðautism in order to initiate treatment as early as possible.ÖHowever, some people do not believe in treatment for autism, ei÷r becauseþy do not believe autism is a disorder or becauseþy believe treatment can do more harmýan good.\n\n===Social development===\nTypically, developing infants are social beings&amp;mdash;early in lifeþy do suchýings as gaze at people, turn toward voices, grasp a finger,íeven smile. In contrast, most autistic children prefer objects to facesíseem to have tremendous difficulty learning to engage inÿgive-and-takeðeveryday human interaction. Even inÿfirst few monthsðlife, many seem indifferent to o÷r people becauseþy avoid eye contactído not interact withþm as often as non-autistic children.\n\nChildren with autism often appear to prefer being alone toÿcompanyðo÷rsímay passively accept suchýings as hugsícuddling without reciprocating, or resist attention altoge÷r. Later,þy seldom seek comfort from o÷rs or respond to parents\' displaysð[[anger]] or [[affection]] in a typical way. Reseã has suggestedýat although autistic children are attached toþir [[parent]]s,þir expression ofýis attachment is unusualídifficult to interpret. Parents who looked forward toÿjoysðcuddling, [[teaching]],íplaying withþir child may feel crushed byýis lackðexpected [[attachmentþory|attachment]] behavior.\n\nChildren with autism appear to lack &quot;[[Theoryðmind|÷oryðmind]]&quot;,ÿability to seeýings fromèo÷r person\'s perspective, a behavior cited as exclusive to human beings aboveÿageðfiveèd, possibly, o÷r higher [[primate]]s such as adult [[gorilla]]s, [[Common chimpanzee|chimpanzee]]sí[[bonobos]]. Typical 5-year-olds can develop insights into o÷r people\'s different knowledge, feelings,íintentions, interpretations based upon social cues (e.g., gestures, facial expressions). An Î with autism seems to lackþse interpretation skills,è inabilityýat leavesþm unable to predict or understand o÷r people\'s actions. Tç[[social alienation]]ðautisticíAsperger\'s people is so intense from childhoodýat many ofþm have [[imaginary friend]]s as companionship. However, havingè imaginary friend is not necessarily a signðautismíalso occurs in non-autistic children. \n\nAlthough not universal, it is common for autistic people to not regulateþir behavior. This can takeÿformðcrying or verbal outburstsýat may seem outðproportion toÿsituation. Individuals with autism generally prefer consistent routinesíenvironments;þy may react negatively to changes inþm. It is not uncommon forþse Îs to exhibit aggression, increased levelsðself-stimulatory behavior, self-injury or extensive withdrawal in overwhelming situations.\n\n===Sensory system===\nA key indicator to clinicians making a proper assessment for autism would include looking for symptoms much likeýose found in [[Sensory Integration Dysfunction|sensory integration dysfunction]]. Children will exhibit problems coping withÿnormal sensory input. Indicators ofýis disorder include oversensitivity or underreactivity to touch, movement, sights, or sounds; physical clumsiness or carelessness; poor body awareness; a tendency to be easily distracted; impulsive physical or verbal behavior;è activity levelýat is unusually high or low; not unwinding or calming oneself; difficulty learning new movements; difficulty in making transitions from one situation toèo÷r; socialèd/or emotional problems; delays in [[Speech delay|speech]], [[Language delay|language]] or [[motor skills]]; specific learning difficulties/delays in academic achievement.\n\nOne common example isè Î with autism [[Hearing (sense)|hearing]]. A person with Autism may have trouble hearing certain people while o÷r people are louderýan usual. Orÿperson with autism may be unable to filter out sounds in certain situations, such as in a large crowdðpeople (see [[cocktail party effect]]). However,ýis is perhapsÿpart ofÿautismýat tends to varyÿmost from person to person, soþse examples may not apply to every autistic.\n\nIt should be notedýat sensory difficulties, although reportedly common in autistics, are not part ofÿ[[DSM-IV]] diagnostic criteria for \'\'autistic disorder\'\'.\n\n===Communication difficulties===\nBy age 3, typical children have passed predictable language learning milestones; one ofÿearliest is babbling. Byÿfirst birthday, a typical toddler says words, turns when çor sçhears his or her name, points when çor sçwants a toy,íwhen offered something distasteful, makes it clearýatÿanswer is &quot;no.&quot; Speech development in people with autism takes different paths.ÖSome remain [[mute]]ýroughoutþir lives while being fully [[literacy|literate]]íable to communicate in o÷r ways&amp;mdash;images, [[sign language]],í[[typing]] are far more natural toþm. Some infants who later show signsðautism cooíbabble duringÿfirst few monthsðlife, but stop soon afterwards. O÷rs may be delayed, developing language as late asÿ[[adolescence|teenage]] years.ÖStill, inability to speak does not meanýat people with autism are unintelligent or unaware.ÖOnce given appropriate accommodations, many will happily converse for hours,ícan often be found in online [[chat room]]s, discussion boards or [[website]]síeven using communication devices at autism-community social events such as [[Autreat]].\n\nThose who do speak often use [[language]] in unusual ways, retaining featuresðearlier stagesðlanguage development for long periods orýroughoutþir lives.ÖSome speak only single words, while o÷rs repeatÿsame phrase overíover. Some repeat whatþy hear, a condition called [[echolalia]].ÖSing-song repetitions in particular are a calming, joyous activityýat many autistic adults engage in.ÖMany people with autism have a strong [[tonality|tonal]] sense,ícan often understand spoken language. \nSome children may exhibit only slight delays in language, or even seem to have precocious languageíunusually large [[vocabulary|vocabularies]], but have great difficulty in sustaining typical [[conversation]]s.ÖTç&quot;giveítake&quot;ðnon-autistic conversation is hard forþm, althoughþy often carry on a [[monologue]] on a favorite subject, giving no one elseè opportunity to comment. When givenÿchance to converse with o÷r autistics,þy comfortably do so in &quot;parallel monologue&quot;&amp;mdash;taking turns expressing viewsíinformation.ÖJust as &quot;[[neurotypical]]s&quot; (people without autism) have trouble understanding autistic [[body language]]s, vocal tones, or phraseology, people with autism similarly have trouble with suchýings in people without autism.ÖIn particular, autistic language abilities tend to be highly literal; people without autism often inappropriately attribute hidden meaning to what people with autism say or expectÿperson with autism to sense such unstated meaning inþir own words.\n\nTçbody languageðpeople with autism can be difficult for o÷r people to understand. Facial expressions, movements,ígestures may be easily understood by some o÷r people with autism, but do not matchýose used by o÷r people. Also,þir toneðvoice has a much more subtle inflection in reflectingþir feelings,èdÿ[[auditory system]]ða person without autism often cannot senseÿfluctuations. What seems to non-autistic people like a high-pitched, sing-song, or flat, [[robot]]-like voice is common in autistic children. Some autistic children with relatively good language skills speak like little adults, ra÷rýan communicating atþir current age level, which is one ofÿthingsýat can lead to problems.\n\nSince non-autistic people are often unfamiliar withÿautistic [[body language]],ísince autistic natural language may not tend towards speech, autistic people often struggle to let o÷r people know whatþy need. Asèybody might do in such a situation,þy may scream in frustration or resort to grabbing whatþy want. While waiting for non-autistic people to learn to communicate withþm, people with autism do whateverþy can to getýrough toþm. Communication difficulties may contribute to autistic people becoming sociallyèxious or depressed.\n\n===Repetitive behaviors===\nAlthough people with autism usually appear physically normalíhave good muscle control, unusual repetitive motions, known as self-stimulation or &quot;stimming,&quot; may setþm apart. These behaviors might be extremeíhighly apparent or more subtle. Some childreníolder Îs spend a lotðtime repeatedly flappingþir arms or wigglingþir toes, o÷rs suddenly freeze in position. As [[child]]ren,þy might spend hours lining upþir carsítrains in a certain way, not usingþm for pretend play. If someone accidentally moves one ofþse toys,ÿchild may be tremendously upset. Autistic children often need,ídemand, absolute consistency inþir environment. A slight change inèy routine&amp;mdash;in mealtimes, dressing, taking a bath, or going to school at a certain timeíbyÿsame route&amp;mdash;can be extremely disturbing. People with autism sometimes have a persistent, intense preoccupation. For example,ÿchild might be obsessed with learning all about [[vacuum cleaners]], [[train]] schedules or [[lighthouses]]. Oftenþy show great interest in different languages, numbers, symbols or [[science]] topics.ÖRepetitive behaviors can also extend intoÿspoken word as well. Perseverationða single word or phrase, even for a specific numberðtimes can also become a part ofÿchild\'s daily routine.\n\n===Effects in education===\nChildren with autism are affected withþse symptoms every day. These unusual characteristics setþm apart fromÿeveryday normal student. Becauseþy have trouble understanding people’sýoughtsífeelings,þy have trouble understanding whatþir teacher may be tellingþm. They do not understandýat facial expressionsívocal variations hold meaningsímay misinterpret what emotionþir instructor is displaying. This inability to fully decipherÿworld aroundþm makes education stressful. Teachers need to be awareða student\'s disorder soýatþy are able to helpÿstudent getÿbest out ofÿlessons being taught. \n\nSome students learn better with visual aids asþy are better able to understand material presentedýis way. Because ofýis, many teachers create “visual schedules” forþir autistic students. This allowsÿstudent to know what is going onýroughoutÿday, soþy know what to prepare foríwhat activityþy will be doing next. Some autistic children have trouble going from one activity toÿnext, soýis visual schedule can help to reduce stress. \n\nReseã has shownýat working in pairs may be beneficial to autistic children. &lt;!-- cite a source here, please! --&gt; Autistic students have problems in schools not only with languageícommunication, but with socialization as well. They feel self-conscious aboutþmselvesímany feelýatþy will always be outcasts. By allowingþm to work with peersþy can make friends, which in turn can helpþm cope withÿproblemsýat arise. By doing soþy can become more integrated intoÿmainstream environment ofÿclassroom.\n\nA teacher\'s aide can also be useful toÿstudent. Tçaide is able to give more elaborate directionsýatÿteacher may not have time to explain toÿautistic child. Tçaide can also facilitateÿautistic child in such a way as to allowþm to stay at a similar level toÿrest ofÿclass. This allows a partially one-on-one lesson structure soýatÿchild is still able to stay in a normal classroom but be givenÿextra helpýatþy need. \n\nThere are many different techniquesýat teachers can use to assistþir students. A teacher needs to become familiar withÿchild’s disorder to know what will work best withýat particular child. Every child is going to be differentíteachers have to be able to adjust with every one ofþm.\n\nStudents with Autism Spectrum Disorders typically have high levelsðanxietyístress, particularly in social environments like school. If a student exhibits aggressive or explosive behavior, it is important for educational teams to recognizeÿimpactðstressíanxiety. Preparing students for new situations by writing Social Stories can lowerèxiety. Teaching socialíemotional concepts using systematic teaching approaches such as TçIncredible 5-Point Scale or o÷r Cognitive Behavioral strategies can increase a student\'s ability to control excessive behavioral reactions.\n\n== DSM definition ==\nAutism is defined in section 299.00 ofÿ[[DiagnosticíStatistical ManualðMental Disorders]] (DSM-IV) as:\n#A totalðsix (or more) items from (1), (2)í(3), with at least two from (1),íone each from (2)í(3): \n##qualitative impairment in social interaction, as manifested by at least two ofÿfollowing: \n###marked impairment inÿuseðmultiple nonverbal behaviors such as eye-to-eye gaze, facial expression, body postures,ígestures to regulate social interaction \n###failure to develop peer relationships appropriate to developmental level \n###a lackðspontaneous seeking to share enjoyment, interests, or achievements with o÷r people (e.g., by a lackðshowing, bringing, or pointing out objectsðinterest) \n###lackðsocial or emotional reciprocity \n##qualitative impairments in communication as manifested by at least one ofÿfollowing: \n###delay in, or total lack of,ÿdevelopmentðspoken language (not accompanied byè attempt to compensateýrough alternative modesðcommunication such as gesture or mime) \n###in Îs with adequate speech, marked impairment inÿability to initiate or sustain a conversation with o÷rs \n###stereotypedírepetitive useðlanguage or idiosyncratic language \n###lackðvaried, spontaneous make-believe play or social imitative play appropriate to developmental level\n##restricted repetitiveístereotyped patternsðbehavior, interests,íactivities, as manifested by at least one ofÿfollowing: \n###encompassing preoccupation with one or more stereotypedírestricted patternsðinterestýat is abnormal ei÷r in intensity or focus \n###apparently inflexible adherence to specific, nonfunctional routines or rituals \n###stereotypedírepetitive motor mannerisms (e.g., hand or finger flapping or twisting, or complex whole-body movements) \n###persistent preoccupation with partsðobjects\n#Delays or abnormal functioning in at least one ofÿfollowing areas, with onset prior to age 3 years: (1) social interaction, (2) language as used in social communication, or (3) symbolic or imaginative play. \n#Tçdisturbance is not better accounted for by [[Rett syndrome|Rett\'s Disorder]] or [[Childhood disintegrative disorder|Childhood Disintegrative Disorder]].\n\nTç\'\'DiagnosticíStatistical Manual\'\'&lt;!-- --&gt;\'s diagnostic criteria in general is controversial for being vagueísubjective.Ö(Seeÿ[[DSM cautionary statement]].) Tçcriteria for autism is much more controversialísome clinicians today may ignore it completely, instead solely relying on o÷r methods for determiningÿdiagnosis.\n\n== Typesðautism == \nAutism presents in a wide degree, fromýose who are nearly [[dysfunctional]]íapparently [[Developmental Disability|mentally handicapped]] toýose whose symptoms are mild or remedied enough to appear unexceptional (&quot;normal&quot;) toÿgeneral public. In termsðboth classificationèdþrapy, autistic Îs are often divided intoýose withè [[Intelligence Quotient|IQ]]&amp;lt;80 referred to as having &quot;low-functioning autism&quot; (LFA), whileýose with IQ&amp;gt;80 are referred to as having &quot;high-functioning autism&quot; (HFA). Lowíhigh functioning are more generally applied to how wellè Î can accomplish activitiesðdaily living, ra÷rýan to [[IQ]].ÖTçterms lowíhigh functioning are controversialínot all autistics acceptþse labels.ÖFur÷r,þse two labels are not currently used or accepted in autism literature.\n\nThis discrepancy can lead to confusion among service providers who equate IQ with functioningímay refuse to serve high-IQ autistic people who are severely compromised inþir ability to perform daily living tasks, or may fail to recognizeÿintellectual potentialðmany autistic people who are considered LFA. For example, some professionals refuse to recognize autistics who can speak or write as being autistic at all, becauseþy stillýinkðautism as a communication disorder so severeýat no speech or writing is possible.\n\nAs a consequence, many &quot;high-functioning&quot; autistic persons,íautistic people with a relatively high [[IQ]], are underdiagnosed,ýus makingÿclaimýat &quot;autism implies retardation&quot; self-fulfilling. Tçnumberðpeople diagnosed with LFA is not rising quite as sharply as HFA, indicatingýat at least part ofÿexplanation forÿapparent rise is probably better diagnostics.\n\n=== Asperger\'síKanner\'s syndrome ===\n[[Image:Hans Asperger.jpg|thumb|right|160px|Asperger described his patients as &quot;little professors&quot;.]]\nInÿcurrent [[DiagnosticíStatistical ManualðMental Disorders]] (DSM-IV-TR),ÿmost significant difference between Autistic Disorder (Kanner\'s)íAsperger\'s syndrome isýat a diagnosis ofÿformer includesÿobservationð&quot;[d]elays or abnormal functioning in at least one ofÿfollowing areas, with onset prior to age 3 years: (1) social interaction, (2) language as used in social communication, or (3) symbolic or imaginative play[,]&quot; {{ref|bnat}} while a diagnosisðAsperger\'s syndrome observes &quot;no clinically significant delay&quot; inþse areas. {{ref|bnas}}\n\nTçDSM makes no mentionðlevelðintellectual functioning, butÿfactýat Asperger\'s autistics as a group tend to perform betterýanýose with Kanner\'s autism has produced a popular conceptionýat \'\'[[Asperger\'s syndrome]]\'\' is synonymous with &quot;higher-functioning autism,&quot; orýat it is a lesser [[disorder]]ýan \'\'autism\'\'.ÖThere is also a popular but not necessarily true conceptionýat all autistic Îs with a high levelðintellectual functioning have Asperger\'s autism orýat both types are merely [[geek]]s with a medical label attached. Also, autism has evolved inÿpublic understanding, butÿpopular identificationðautism with relatively severe cases as accurately depicted in \'\'[[Rain Man]]\'\' has encouraged relativesðfamily members diagnosed inÿautistic spectrum to speak ofþir loved ones as having Asperger\'s syndrome ra÷rýan autism.\n\n===Autism as a spectrum disorder===\n{{details|Autistic spectrum}}\n\nAno÷r view ofþse disorders isýatþy are on a continuum known as [[autistic spectrum]] disorders.ÖA related continuum is [[Sensory Integration Dysfunction]], which is about how well we integrateÿinformation we receive from our senses.ÖAutism, Asperger\'s syndrome,íSensory Integration Dysfunction are all closely relatedíoverlap.\n\nThere are two main manifestationsðclassical autism, [[regressive autism]]í[[early infantile autism]].ÖEarly infantile autism is present at birth while regressive autism begins beforeÿageð3íoften around 18 months.ÖAlthoughýis causes some controversy over whenÿneurological differences involved in autism truly begin, some believeýat it is only a matterðwhenè environmental toxin triggersÿdisorder. This triggering could occur during gestation due to a toxinýat entersÿmo÷r\'s bodyíis transfered toÿfetus. Tçtriggering could also occur after birth duringÿcrucial early nervous system development ofÿchild due to a toxin directly enteringÿchild\'s body.\n\n== Increase in diagnosesðautism ==\n{{details|Autism epidemic}}\n\n[[Image:autismnocgraph.png|right|thumb|400px|Tçnumberðreported casesðautism has increased dramatically overÿpast decade. Statistics in graph fromÿ[[National Center for Health Statistics]].]]\nThere has beenè explosion worldwide in reported casesðautism overÿlast ten years, which is largely reminiscentðincreases inÿdiagnosisð[[schizophrenia]]í[[multiple personality disorder]] inÿtwentieth century. This has brought rise to a numberðdifferentþories as toÿnature ofÿsudden increase.\n\nEpidemiologists argueýatÿrise in diagnoses inÿUnited States is partly or entirely attributable to changes in diagnostic criteria, reclassifications, public awareness,èdÿincentive to receive federally mandated services. A widely cited study fromÿ[[M.I.N.D. Institute]] in California ([[17 October]] [[2002]]), claimedýatÿincrease in autism is real, even afterýose complicating factors are accounted for (see reference inýis section below).\n\nO÷r reseãers remain unconvinced (see references below), including Dr. Chris Johnson, a professorðpediatrics atÿUniversityðTexas Health Sciences Center at [[San Antonio]]ícochair ofÿ[[American AcademyðPediatrics]] Autism Expert Panel, who says, &quot;There is a chance we\'re seeing a true rise, but right now I don\'týinkèybody canèswerýat question for sure.&quot; ([[Newsweek]] reference below).\n\nTheèswer toýis question has significant ramifications onÿdirectionðreseã, since a \'\'real increase\'\' would focus more attention (and reseã funding) onÿseã for environmental factors, while \'\'little or no real increase\'\' would focus more attention to genetics. Onÿo÷r hand, it is conceivableýat certain environmental factors (vaccination, diet, societal changes) may have a particular impact on people with a specific genetic constitution. There is little public reseã onÿeffectsð[[in vitro fertilization]] onÿnumberðincidencesðautism.\n\nOne ofÿmore popularþories isýatþre is a connection between &quot;geekdom&quot;íautism.ÖThis is hinted, for instance, by a \'\'Wired Magazine\'\' article in 2001 entitled &quot;Tç[[Geek]] Syndrome&quot;, which is a point argued by many inÿautism rights movement{{ref|Wired}}.ÖThis article, many professionals assert, is just one example ofÿmedia\'s applicationðmental disease labels to what is actually variant normal behavior&amp;mdash;÷y argueýat shyness, lackðathletic ability or social skills,íintellectual interests, even whenþy seem unusual to o÷rs, are not inþmselves signsðautism or Asperger\'s syndrome. O÷rs assertýat it is actuallyÿmedical profession which is applying mental disease labels to children who inÿpast would have simply been accepted as a little different or even labeled \'gifted\'. See [[clinomorphism]] for fur÷r discussion ofýis issue.\n\nDue toÿrecent publicity surrounding autismíautistic spectrum disorders,è increasing numberðadults are choosing to seek diagnosesðhigh-functioning autism or Asperger\'s syndrome in lightðsymptomsþy currently experience or experienced during childhood. Sinceÿcauseðautism isýought to be at least partly genetic, a proportion ofþse adults seekþir own diagnosis specifically as follow-up toþir children\'s diagnoses. Because autism falls intoÿ[[pervasive developmental disorder]] category, strictly speaking, symptoms must have been present in a given patient before age seven in order to make a [[differential diagnosis]].\n\n== Therapies ==\n{{details|Autismþrapies}}\n\n==Sociology==\nDue toÿcomplexityðautism,þre are many facetsð[[sociology]]ýat need to be considered when discussing it, such asÿculture which has evolved from autistic persons connectingícommunicating with oneèo÷r.ÖIn addition,þre are several subgroups forming withinÿautistic community, sometimes in strong opposition to oneèo÷r.\n\n===Communityípolitics===\n{{details|Autistic community}}\n{{details|Autism rights movement}}\n\nMuch like many o÷r controversies inÿworld,ÿautistic community itself has splintered off into several groups.ÖEssentially,þse groups areýose who seek a cure for autism, dubbed \'\'pro-cure\'\',ýose who do not desire a cure for autismías such resist it, dubbed \'\'anti-cure\'\',èdÿmany people caught inÿmiddle ofÿtwo.ÖIn recent history, with scientists learning more about autismípossibly coming closer to a cure, some members ofÿ&quot;anti-cure&quot; movement [[Autistic community#Declaration fromÿautism community|sent a letter toÿUnited Nations]] demanding to be treated as a minority group ra÷rýan a group with a [[mental disability]] or disease. Websites such asÖautistics.org{{ref|refbot.770}} presentÿview ofÿanti-cure group.\n\nThere are numerous resources available for autistics from many groups.ÖDue toÿfactýat many autistics find it easier to communicate onlineýan in person, many ofþse resources are available online.ÖIn addition, sometimes successful autistic adults in a local community will help out children with autism, much inÿway a master would help outè apprentice, for example.\n\n2002 was declared [[Autism Awareness Year]] inÿ[[United Kingdom]]&amp;mdash;this idea was initiated by [[IvaníCharika Corea]], parentsðan autistic child, Charin. Autism Awareness Year was led byÿ[[British InstituteðBrain Injured Children]],Ö[[Disabilities Trust]],Ö[[National Autistic Society]],Ö[[Autism London]]í800 organizations inÿUnited Kingdom. It hadÿpersonal backingð[[United Kingdom|British]] Prime MinisterÖ[[Tony Blair]]íparliamentariansðall parties inÿ[[PalaceðWestminster]].\n\n===Culture===\n{{details|Autistic culture}}\nWithÿrecent increases in autism recognitionínew approaches to educatingísocializing autistics,è \'\'autistic culture\'\' has begun to develop.ÖSimilar to [[deaf culture]], autistic culture is based in a beliefýat autism is a unique wayðbeingínot a disorder to be cured.ÖThere are some commonalities which are specific to autism in general as a culture, not just &quot;autistic culture&quot;.\n\nIt is a common misperceptionýat people with autism do not marry; many do get married.ÖOften,þy marryèo÷r person with autism, althoughýis is not alwaysÿcase.ÖMany times autistics are attracted to o÷r autistics due to shared interests or obsessions, but more oftenýan notÿattraction is due to simple compatibility with personality types,ÿsame as is true for non-autistics. Autistics who communi'
